    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d692-a2">

                <pb facs="1043" n="915" sameAs="frus:frus1885/pg915"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2 in No. 88.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bird to Mr.
                            Baker</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consulate</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">City of
                                Caracas</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-05-04"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 4,
                        1885</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 44.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: As supplementary to the official
                    information in the matter of damages to the American schooner Lanie Cobb,
                    communicated to you in dispatch No. 43, of date April 25 ultimo, from the United
                    States consulate at La Guayra, I have to state that I have <pb facs="1044"
                        n="916" xml:id="pg916"/> been informed, and believe, that the master of the
                    Lanie Cobb, at some trifling expense, recovered his anchor and chain before
                    sailing from the port of La Guayra, which, in the estimate of repairs, were
                    valued in the sum of $878. All the official information in this case, as
                    furnished you in the above mentioned dispatch, was promptly transmitted to the
                    Department of State at Washington.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>WINFIELD S. BIRD,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">United States
                            Consul.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
